https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Standing Advisory Council on Religious Education","time":"6:30 pm","status":"Confirmed; Annual Meeting","webcast":true,"url":"https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Health Overview and Scrutiny Panel","time":"7:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-12","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","time":"7:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-09","committee":"Health Overview and Scrutiny Panel","time":"7:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-14","committee":"Council","time":"7:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-14","committee":"Council","time":"8:30 pm","status":"New; Special Meeting at the rising of Council","webcast":true,"url":"https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-15","committee":"Pension Board","time":"10:00 am","status":"Cancelled","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Audit, Governance and Standards Committee","time":"7:30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://moderngov.kingston.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Kingston pension fund faces software crisis blocking legal deadline","lead":"Kingston upon Thames's pension fund is racing against a 1 January 2028 statutory deadline to recalculate benefits for 3,483 members under a legally required reform, but faces a critical software dependency with no confirmed delivery dates. The core system was delivered in July 2025 but defects prevent accurate benefit calculations. The supplier has said patches will fix the issue, but dates remain unconfirmed. Officers rate this a red risk (the highest level) because the work cannot proceed without the patches. The council is using a standalone calculator to ensure members retiring or dying immediately have correct benefits calculated while waiting. The delay stems from industry-wide defects affecting multiple public sector pension funds pressing suppliers for fixes.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"7a. Annex A - Governance and Risk Update_ Pension Fund Panel _ 20260923","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115145/115145.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Revenue &amp; Capital Budget Monitoring 2026_27 as at Month 4","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115198/115198.pdf","headline":"On-street parking income \u00a30.47m below budget due to demand stabilization post-tariff changes, indicating structural revenue loss","detail":"The council is forecasting a \u00a30.47m adverse variance on on-street parking income. The council attributes this to 'stabilisation in rates and usage following the pricing changes introduced in 2025/26', indicating that demand has adjusted downward and plateaued at a lower level rather than recovering. Bus lane enforcement and moving traffic contravention income are lower than the prior year but currently in line with budget at \u00a30.00m variance, with improved compliance at Kingston Road offsetting the impact of reduced enforcement hours at Crescent Road. This suggests enforcement income is also stabilizing at lower levels.","category":"income","matters":"Parking income is a discretionary revenue that the council cannot easily grow. A structural reduction in both volume and tariff indicates the council has less flexibility to increase this income stream in future. Combined with other income shortfalls (Land Charges \u00a30.07m, advertising income \u00a30.30m delayed), this points to recurring income volatility.","figures":"\u00a30.47m on-street parking adverse variance; rates and usage stabilized at lower levels following 2025/26 pricing changes; enforcement income in line with budget (\u00a30.00m variance)","quote":"The main variance in resources is an adverse variance of \u00a30.47m in the on-street parking account. This reflects the stabilisation in rates and usage following the pricing changes introduced in 2025/26.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Revenue &amp; Capital Budget Monitoring 2026_27 as at Month 4","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115198/115198.pdf","headline":"Adult Social Care Care Packages overspending by \u00a30.76m driven by provider cost challenges and \u00a30.3m health partner income shortfall","detail":"ASC Care Packages are forecasting a \u00a30.76m overspend. The main driver is market pressure: 'a high number of providers have challenged the uplift awarded for care package costs' and commissioning staff are continuing negotiations with \u00a30.5m in costs estimated pending conclusion. Additionally, health partner income is falling short by 'just under \u00a30.3m' as budgeted amounts exceed what will actually be received, with figures for the previous year only recently confirmed. These pressures persist despite the council's transformation programme, which is helping to manage demand through greater focus on prevention and home support rather than residential placements.","category":"overspend","matters":"Care provider cost inflation driven by market competition for staff is a structural risk unlikely to be reversed. The health income gap suggests underlying funding misalignment that may persist or worsen. Combined, these represent an ongoing cost pressure that may not be controllable through efficiency measures alone.","figures":"\u00a30.76m ASC Care Packages overspend; \u00a30.5m pending provider cost negotiations; \u00a30.3m health income shortfall; 1,600+ adults with long-term care needs supported","quote":"Market pressures are the main contributing factor for the projected overspend as a high number of providers have challenged the uplift awarded for care package costs. Income from Health Partners is an ongoing challenge, with the figures for the previous financial year (2025/26) only recently confirmed. There is a pressure of just under \u00a30.3m as the budgeted amounts are higher than will actually be received according to the latest forecasts.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Revenue &amp; Capital Budget Monitoring 2026_27 as at Month 4","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115198/115198.pdf","headline":"General Fund capital programme underspending by \u00a323.32m at Month 4 with only 25% of forecast spent, multiple major projects slipped and 1-4-1 scheme \u00a34m stalled","detail":"The General Fund capital programme has forecast spend of \u00a394.94m against a budget of \u00a3118.26m, an underspend of \u00a323.32m. Spend is running at only 25% of forecast at Month 4 (13% for HRA), indicating significant slippage. Major project deferrals include: CRE property buybacks (\u00a33.89m rephased), VA Church of England School (\u00a32.5m cancelled by DfE), AFC Post 16 Campus (\u00a32.0m rephased pending site confirmation), and Fully Integrated Family Hub (\u00a31.7m delayed until December 2027 construction start). The 1-4-1 Developments show \u00a34.0m underspend because the housing association delivering Bonner Hill Road and Roselands schemes has 'encountered problems including loss of their development contractors', with ongoing negotiations required.","category":"capital","matters":"Capital underspend indicates investment is not being delivered as planned. Low spend rates at Month 4 combined with repeated slippages across multiple schemes suggest persistent programme delivery challenges. Dependency on partner organisations (housing associations) creates recovery risk if delivery partners do not resolve contractor issues.","figures":"\u00a323.32m General Fund in-year underspend (\u00a331.61m including HRA); 25% of GF forecast spent at Month 4; \u00a33.89m CRE rephase; \u00a32.5m VA CoE cancelled; \u00a34.0m 1-4-1 stalled; multiple other project deferrals","quote":"The Capital Programme is forecasting an in-year underspend of \u00a331.61m for 2026/27. Actual expenditure is currently standing at 25% of the forecast for general fund projects and 13% for the HRA programme. 1-4-1 Developments - An underspend of \u00a34.00m is reported for this capital programme. The housing association delivering those schemes has encountered problems including loss of their development contractors. Discussions are ongoing with the housing association for a release of the unused receipts.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Revenue &amp; Capital Budget Monitoring 2026_27 as at Month 4","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115198/115198.pdf","headline":"Schools DSG forecast overspend of \u00a318.18m with cumulative deficit rising to \u00a338.72m, addressed by new High Needs Stability Grant but future funding uncertain","detail":"The council is forecasting \u00a3235.61m spend on DSG services against \u00a3219.23m funding, an in-year underfunding of \u00a318.18m. The High Needs Block is overspending by \u00a318.42m, driven by rising demand (2,236 EHCPs at end June 2026, up 8% year-on-year) against frozen government funding. The cumulative deficit is projected at \u00a338.72m by year-end. The DfE has introduced a new High Needs Stability Grant expected to write off 90% of the prior-year deficit (\u00a320.52m at March 2026), subject to SEND Reform Plan approval (expected September 2026). However, the DfE has made clear that future support 'will not be unlimited' and there is 'not yet clarity' on support levels for 2026/27 and 2027/28 deficits.","category":"demand","matters":"A \u00a338.72m potential deficit represents a major contingent liability. Although the new grant will write off most of the prior-year deficit, uncertainty about future years' support (described as not unlimited and contingent on delivering transformation) creates medium-term budget risk. The structural driver\u2014rising EHCP demand against frozen funding\u2014is unresolved.","figures":"\u00a318.18m in-year overspend; \u00a338.72m cumulative deficit before grant; 2,236 EHCPs (8% increase year-on-year); High Needs Block overspend \u00a318.42m; government funding frozen at 2025/26 levels","quote":"The council is projected to spend \u00a3235.61m, including \u00a31.805m future demand, on DSG funded education services in 2026/27 compared to a grant allocation of \u00a3219.23m (including recoupment). This represents an in-year underfunding of \u00a318.18m. There is not yet clarity about whether the DfE will provide similar levels of support for deficits that may accumulate in 2026/27 and 2027/28. They have confirmed that they will take 'an appropriate and proportionate approach' but that it will not be 'unlimited'.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Corporate and Resources Committee","paper_title":"Revenue &amp; Capital Budget Monitoring 2026_27 as at Month 4","paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115198/115198.pdf","headline":"Savings target only 68% delivered in-year (\u00a32.34m of \u00a33.43m), with \u00a30.28m marked Red and just \u00a30.22m mitigations identified against \u00a30.50m shortfall","detail":"The council budgeted \u00a33.43m savings for 2026/27. At Month 4, \u00a32.34m (68%) is forecast to be delivered in-year and \u00a30.87m (25%) delayed to future years, totalling 93% expected delivery. However, \u00a30.28m (8%) is marked Red with no path to delivery. The council has identified \u00a30.22m mitigations to offset the shortfall but reports that 'Further mitigation measures will be sought', indicating a \u00a30.06m gap that remains unresolved. Key unachievable items include \u00a30.05m Promoting Independence savings in Adult Social Care and \u00a30.05m+ in events and contract rationalisation income.","category":"savings","matters":"Budget plans depend on delivering planned savings. Where only 68% is achieved in-year and 7% cannot be delivered at all, this raises questions about the realism of the financial plan and the council's ability to execute savings. Reliance on future mitigation measures rather than identified actions suggests financial flexibility is being eroded.","figures":"\u00a33.43m total savings target; \u00a32.34m (68%) in-year delivery; \u00a30.87m (25%) delayed; \u00a30.28m (8%) Red; \u00a30.22m mitigations identified","quote":"\u00a32.34m (68%) of agreed savings are forecast to be delivered in 2026/27 with a further \u00a30.87m (25%) of savings which are expected to be delivered but will be delayed giving overall savings expected to be achieved of \u00a33.19m (93%). \u00a30.22m of savings mitigations have been identified to offset the delayed and non-deliverable savings. Further mitigation measures will be sought.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-23"},"name":"Kingston Upon Thames","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-23","since_iso":"2026-08-24","distress":false,"headline":"Panel governance capacity temporarily impaired by post-election membership changes despite induction training.","lead":"Effective pension fund governance requires Panel members to possess sufficient skills and knowledge to challenge officers and advisors; temporary capacity loss increases reliance on officers and external advisors.","also":["28 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Corporate and Resources Committee on 2026-09-24","n_findings":29,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"7. Governance and Risk Update_ Pension Fund Panel _ 20260923","top_paper_url":"https://kingston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s115152/115152.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":127,"news_drafts_total":1}}